"come from §3.  Then §4."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `A : Set U`
#. §1: `Aᶜ`
#. §2: `U`
#. §3: `A`
#. §4: `x : U`
#. §5: `x ∈ Aᶜ`
#. §6: `x ∉ A`
#. §7: `x ∈ Aᶜ ↔ x ∉ A`
#. §8: `↔`
#. §9: `\\iff`
#. §10: `c`
#. §11: `\\compl`
#. §12: `\\^c`
#. §13: `x ∈ Aᶜ ↔ x ∉ A`
#. §14: `rfl`
#. §15: `rfl`
#. §16: `P ↔ Q`
#. §17: `P`
#. §18: `Q`
#. §19: `P`
#. §20: `Q`
#. §21: `rfl`
#. §22: `X = Y`
#. §23: `X`
#. §24: `Y`
#: Game.Levels.Comp.L02compdef
msgid "If you have §0, then §1 is defined to be the set of all objects in the universe §2\n"
"that are not elements of §3.  That means that if you also have §4, then the statements\n"
"§5 and §6 are equivalent.  We express this by saying that the statement\n"
"§7 is true.  (The symbol §8 means \"if and only if\", and you can enter it\n"
"by typing §9.  You can enter the superscript §10 in the notation for the complement\n"
"of a set by typing §11 or §12.)\n"
"\n"
"In this level, we're going to prove that the statement §13 is true, and to do\n"
"it we'll use a new tactic: §14.  The §15 tactic can prove any statement of the form\n"
"§16 if §17 and §18 are statements that are equivalent by virtue of the definitions of\n"
"the symbols occurring in them.  (We say in this case that §19 and §20 are *definitionally\n"
"equivalent*.)  The §21 tactic can also prove statements of the form §22, if §23 and\n"
"§24 are definitionally equal--that is, equal by virtue of definitions."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rewrite`
