#. §0: `rewrite`
#. §1: `rewrite`
#: Game.Levels.Inter.L06inter_sub_swap
msgid "It will help you see how to proceed if you\n"
"write out the definition of intersection in both the assumption «{h}» and the goal.\n"
"Using the §0 tactic isn't necessary; you can just do the rewriting in\n"
"your head rather than asking Lean to do it.  But if it helps you to figure out the\n"
"proof, go ahead and use the §1 tactic."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.FamCombo.L02compinter
msgid "Perhaps you have already guessed that there is a theorem about the complement\n"
"of an intersection of a family that is similar to the theorem in the last level."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.FamInter.L06eltwiseunion
msgid "Writing out the meaning of the goal will make the proof easier to understand."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `x`
#. §1: `x ∈ A`
#: Game.Levels.Subset.L06subtrans
msgid "To get started, you'll need to introduce first\n"
"an object §0 and then the assumption that §1."
msgstr ""

#: GameServer.RpcHandlers
msgid "level completed! 🎉"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `exact`
#. §1: `t`
#. §2: `exact t`
#: Game.Levels.Subset.L01exact
msgid "Use §0 to close a goal.  If some expression §1 is a proof of\n"
"the goal, then §2 will close the goal.\n"
"\n"
"Think of \"exact\" as meaning \"this is exactly what is needed to prove the goal.\""
msgstr ""

#. §0: `apply Or.inl`
#. §1: `apply Or.inr`
#. §2: `left`
#. §3: `right`
#: Game.Levels.FamInter.L06eltwiseunion
msgid "For the second case, which half of the goal do you think you should try to prove?\n"
"You can use §0 or §1 (or the equivalent tactics §2 or §3)\n"
"to specify what goal you're going to prove."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `have`
#. §1: `t`
#. §2: `P`
#. §3: `h`
#. §4: `have h : P := t`
#. §5: `h : P`
#. §6: `have`
#. §7: `P`
#. §8: `P`
#. §9: `have h : P`
#. §10: `P`
#. §11: `P`
#. §12: `P`
#. §13: `h : P`
#. §14: `have h := t`
#. §15: `P`
#. §16: `t`
#: Game.Levels.Subset.L03have
msgid "Use §0 to assert a statement that you can prove from your current\n"
"assumptions.  You must give the new assertion an identifier; be sure to\n"
"use an identifier that is different from those already in use.\n"
"\n"
"If some expression §1 is a proof of a statement §2, and §3 is an\n"
"identifier that is not in use, then §4 will add §5\n"
"to the list of assumptions.\n"
"\n"
"There are two variations on the §6 tactic:\n"
"\n"
"* Sometimes you want to assert a statement §7, but the proof of §8 is too\n"
"difficult to be given in one line.  In that situation, you can simply write\n"
"§9.  Of course, you must still justify the assertion of §10, so\n"
"the proof of §11 becomes your immediate goal.\n"
"Once the goal of proving §12 has been closed, you will be able to return to\n"
"your original goal, with §13 added to the assumption list.\n"
"\n"
"* If you write §14, then Lean will try to figure out what statement\n"
"§15 is proven by the expression §16 and, if it can figure it out, it will fill\n"
"it in for you."
msgstr ""

#. §0: $F$
#. §1: $A \\in F$
#. §2: $\\bigcap F \\subseteq A$
#: Game.Levels.FamInter.L01intersub
#: Game.Levels.FamUnion.L02subunion
msgid "Suppose §0 is a family of sets and §1.  Then §2."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `A ⊆ B`
#. §1: `x`
#. §2: `A`
#. §3: `B`
#. §4: `x`
#. §5: `x`
#. §6: `A`
#. §7: `A ⊆ A`
#: Game.Levels.Subset.L05subref
msgid "How do you prove that one set is a subset of another?  To prove that §0,\n"
"you have to show that if some object §1 is an element of §2, then it is also\n"
"an element of §3.  To do that, you'll have to introduce an object called §4 into\n"
"the proof.  The object denoted by §5 could be anything, so we say that it\n"
"is *arbitrary*.\n"
"\n"
"In this level, we start with a simple example of this kind of proof.  We're going\n"
"to show that if §6 is a set, then §7."
msgstr ""

#. §0: $A \\subseteq B$
#. §1: $B \\subseteq C$
#. §2: $x \\in A$
#. §3: $x \\in C$
#: Game.Levels.Subset.L03have
msgid "Suppose §0, §1, and §2.  Then §3."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P x`
#. §1: `x`
#. §2: `{x | P x}`
#. §3: `x`
#. §4: `P x`
#. §5: `{x | x ∈ A ∧ x ∈ B}`
#. §6: `A ∩ B`
#. §7: `mem_setOf`
#. §8: `a ∈ {x | P x} ↔ P a`
#. §9: `rewrite [mem_setOf]`
#. §10: `a ∈ {x | P x}`
#. §11: `P a`
#: Game.Levels.FamUnion.L07eltwiseinter
msgid "In this level, we introduce another way to define sets.  If §0 is a statement about an\n"
"unspecified object §1, then §2 denotes the set of all values of §3 that make §4\n"
"come out true.  This is often called *set-builder notation*.  For example,\n"
"§5 is another way to write §6.\n"
"\n"
"As usual, we have a theorem that states the meaning of set-builder notation.  Lean will\n"
"recognize §7 as a proof of any statement of the form §8.\n"
"And that means that §9 will rewrite §10 as §11."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.FamInter.L04interunion
msgid "Intersection of a union of families"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `«{h1}»`
#: Game.Levels.Comp.L04compcomp
msgid "Now write out the definition of complement in §0."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `x`
#. §1: `intro x`
#. §2: `intro`
#. §3: `intro`
#: Game.Levels.Subset.L05subref
msgid "Our first step is to introduce an object §0 into the proof.  To do this, type §1.\n"
"We have already seen that the §2 tactic can be used to introduce a new *assumption* into a\n"
"proof.  This step illustrates a second use of §3: introducing a new *object* into a proof."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `«{h}»`
#: Game.Levels.Union.L04union_sub_swap
msgid "The form of the assumption §0 now suggests proof by cases."
msgstr ""

#. §0: $F$
#. §1: $(\\bigcup F)^c = \\bigcap \\{s \\mid s^c \\in F\\}$
#: Game.Levels.FamCombo.L01compunion
#: Game.Levels.FamCombo.L02compinter
msgid "For any family of sets §0, §1."
msgstr ""

#. §0: $A$
#. §1: $S$
#. §2: $S \\subseteq A$
#: Game.Levels.FamUnion.L01proveexists
msgid "Suppose §0 is a set.  Then there is some set §1 such that §2."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `h1`
#: Game.Levels.FamInter.L06eltwiseunion
msgid "Now use §0."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `h`
#. §1: `P ∨ Q`
#. §2: `rcases h with h1 | h2`
#. §3: `h1 : P`
#. §4: `h2 : Q`
#. §5: `rcases`
#. §6: `P ∨ Q`
#. §7: `rcases`
#: Game.Levels.Union.L03cases
msgid "If §0 is a proof of a statement of the form §1, then the tactic\n"
"§2 will break your proof into cases.  In case 1, you'll have the new\n"
"assumption §3, and in case 2 you'll have §4.  In both cases you have to prove\n"
"the original goal.\n"
"\n"
"The §5 tactic has other uses.  In particular, it can be applied to proofs of statements\n"
"that do not have the form §6.  However, we will not discuss these other uses of the\n"
"§7 tactic in this game."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `«{h3}»`
#. §1: `rcases «{h3}» with «{h3}»A | «{h3}»B`
#: Game.Levels.Union.L03cases
msgid "Now the assumption §0 is an \"or\" statement.  The easiest way to use such an\n"
"assumption is to break your proof into cases.  To do this in Lean, use the tactic\n"
"§1."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `A`
#. §1: `B`
#. §2: `C`
#. §3: `inter_distrib_left A B C`
#. §4: `A ∩ (B ∪ C) = (A ∩ B) ∪ (A ∩ C)`
#. §5: `Set.inter_distrib_left`
#: Game.Levels.Combo.L03inter_distrib_union
#: Game.Levels.Combo.L04union_distrib_inter
msgid "For any sets §0, §1, and §2, §3 is a proof of the\n"
"statement §4.  In Mathlib, the name of this theorem\n"
"is §5."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P ↔ Q`
#. §1: `Iff.intro`
#. §2: `h1 : P → Q`
#. §3: `h2 : Q → P`
#. §4: `Iff.intro h1 h2`
#. §5: `P ↔ Q`
#. §6: `apply Iff.intro`
#. §7: `P → Q`
#. §8: `Q → P`
#: Game.Levels.Comp.L05compsubiff
msgid "In this last level of Complement World, you'll prove a statement of the form §0.  The most\n"
"useful theorem for this purpose is §1.  If you have §2 and §3, then\n"
"§4 is a proof of §5.  As we saw in the last level, that means you can start your\n"
"proof with §6.  Lean will set §7 and §8 as the goals that are needed\n"
"to complete the proof."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Combo
msgid "Combination World"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `A`
#. §1: `U`
#. §2: `A`
#. §3: `Aᶜ`
#. §4: `U`
#. §5: `A`
#. §6: `U`
#. §7: `A`
#. §8: `Aᶜ`
#: Game.Levels.Comp
msgid "If §0 is a set of objects from the universe §1, then the *complement* of §2,\n"
"denoted §3, is the set of all objects in the universe §4 that are *not*\n"
"elements of §5.\n"
"\n"
"For example, if §6 consists of all people and §7 is the set of people who are more than\n"
"6 feet tall, then §8 is the set of all people who are at most 6 feet tall.\n"
"\n"
"In this world, we'll see how to prove theorems about complements."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `«{h2}» «{t}» «{h4}»`
#: Game.Levels.FamInter.L02intersubinter
msgid "§0 is now a proof of the goal."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P ↔ Q`
#. §1: `P`
#. §2: `Q`
#. §3: `rfl`
#. §4: `X = Y`
#. §5: `X`
#. §6: `Y`
#: Game.Levels.Comp.L02compdef
msgid "If your goal is a statement of the form §0, and §1 and §2 are definitionally\n"
"equivalent (that is, equivalent by virtue of the definitions of the symbols occurring in\n"
"them), then the §3 tactic will close the goal.  It will also close a goal of the form\n"
"§4, if §5 and §6 are definitionally equal (that is, equal by virtue of definitions)."
msgstr ""

#: Game
msgid "In this game you will learn the basics of theorem proving in Lean by proving\n"
"theorems about unions, intersections, and complements of sets."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `⋃₀ F`
#. §1: `F`
#. §2: `⋃₀`
#. §3: `\\U0`
#: Game.Levels.FamUnion.L02subunion
msgid "§0 is the union of the family of sets §1.  To enter the symbol §2, type §3."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `A ∩ B ∩ C = A ∩ (B ∩ C)`
#. §1: `(A ∩ B) ∩ C = A ∩ (B ∩ C)`
#. §2: `ext x`
#: Game.Levels.Inter.L08inter_assoc
msgid "Notice that Lean has written the goal as §0, with no\n"
"parentheses on the left.  When an intersection of more than two sets is written\n"
"without parentheses, Lean groups the intersections to the left, so this means\n"
"§1.\n"
"\n"
"To start this proof, use the tactic §2."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `obtain`
#. §1: `«{h1}»`
#: Game.Levels.FamUnion.L04unionpair
msgid "Remember, you can use §0 to introduce a name for the set that is asserted to\n"
"exist in §1."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.FamCombo.L08singleton
msgid "Congratulations!  You have completed the Set Theory Game!\n"
"\n"
"If you want to learn more about Lean, check out the [Lean Community](https://leanprover-community.github.io)."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `A ∪ B ∪ C`
#. §1: `(A ∪ B) ∪ C`
#: Game.Levels.Union.L06union_assoc
msgid "Notice that, as with intersections, Lean groups unions to the left, so\n"
"§0 means §1."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P`
#. §1: `by_cases h : P`
#. §2: `h : P`
#. §3: `h : ¬P`
#. §4: `P`
#: Game.Levels.FamInter.L06eltwiseunion
msgid "In this level you'll need a new kind of proof by cases.  For any statement §0, the\n"
"tactic §1 will break the proof into two cases.  In the first case, the new\n"
"assumption §2 is added to the list of assumptions, and in second, the new\n"
"assumption §3 is added.  Since §4 must be either true or false, these two cases cover\n"
"all possibilities."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `h1 : P → Q`
#. §1: `h2 : Q → P`
#. §2: `Iff.intro h1 h2`
#. §3: `P ↔ Q`
#: Game.Levels.Comp.L05compsubiff
#: Game.Levels.Inter.L04proveand
msgid "If you have §0 and §1, then §2 is a proof of §3."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rewrite`
#. §1: `rewrite [mem_compl_iff]`
#. §2: `mem_compl_iff`
#. §3: `mem_compl_iff`
#. §4: `A`
#. §5: `«{x}»`
#. §6: `rewrite [mem_compl_iff] at «{h2}»`
#. §7: `«{h2}»`
#. §8: `mem_compl_iff`
#. §9: `B`
#. §10: `«{x}»`
#: Game.Levels.Comp.L03compsub
msgid "The §0 tactic is smart enough to figure out some things on its own.  If you\n"
"had just written §1, then Lean would have figured out how to apply the\n"
"theorem §2 to get an equivalence that could be used to make a replacement in the goal.\n"
"In other words, it would have figured out that the theorem §3 had to be applied to\n"
"§4 and §5.\n"
"\n"
"Similarly, you can write §6 to write out the meaning of §7.  Lean\n"
"will figure out that in this case, §8 has to be applied to §9 and §10."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Inter.L02elt_inter_elt_right
msgid "Element of an intersection"
msgstr ""

#. §0: $F$
#. §1: $\\bigcap F$
#. §2: `F`
#. §3: `⋂₀ F`
#. §4: `⋂₀`
#. §5: `\\I0`
#. §6: `F : Set (Set U)`
#. §7: `x : U`
#. §8: `x ∈ ⋂₀ F`
#. §9: `t`
#. §10: `t`
#. §11: `F`
#. §12: `x ∈ t`
#. §13: `∀ t, t ∈ F → x ∈ t`
#. §14: `∀ t ∈ F, x ∈ t`
#. §15: `∀`
#. §16: `\\forall`
#. §17: `∀ t, t ∈ F → x ∈ t`
#. §18: `∀ t, (t ∈ F → x ∈ t)`
#. §19: `(∀ t, t ∈ F) → x ∈ t`
#. §20: `mem_sInter`
#. §21: `x ∈ ⋂₀ F ↔ ∀ t ∈ F, x ∈ t`
#: Game.Levels.FamInter.L01intersub
msgid "In mathematical writing, the intersection of the family §0 is usually denoted §1.\n"
"In Lean, the intersection of a family §2 is denoted §3.  (You can enter the symbol\n"
"§4 by typing §5.)\n"
"\n"
"Suppose we have §6 and §7.  Then §8 means that for every set §9, if\n"
"§10 is in §11, then §12.  To write this statement in Lean, we write §13.\n"
"Lean abbreviates this statement as §14.\n"
"The symbol §15 is called the *universal quantifier*, and you can enter it in Lean by typing\n"
"§16.  Note that §17 means §18, not\n"
"§19.  In other words, Lean interprets the universal quantifier as applying\n"
"to the entire rest of the statement.  If you want it to apply to less, you have to\n"
"use parentheses to indicate that.\n"
"\n"
"As with other set theory operations, we have a theorem that expresses this definition.  Lean will\n"
"recognize §20 as a proof of any statement of the form §21.\n"
"\n"
"In this level, you'll try out these ideas."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `h2 : x ∉ B`
#. §1: `h2 : ¬x ∈ B`
#. §2: `x ∈ B`
#. §3: `x ∈ B → False`
#. §4: `«{h4}» : x ∈ B`
#. §5: `exact h2 «{h4}»`
#: Game.Levels.Comp.L01contra
msgid "You can think of §0 (which is shorthand for §1)\n"
"as meaning \"if §2 were true, then that would lead to a contradiction\"--in other\n"
"words, §3.\n"
"Applying this to your new assumption §4 will give the contradiction\n"
"you need.  In other words, §5 will close the goal."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.Union.L02subunion
msgid "Subset of a union"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `«{ht}»`
#. §1: `have «{ht}»l := «{ht}».left`
#: Game.Levels.FamUnion.L07eltwiseinter
msgid "You can separate out the first half of §0 with §1."
msgstr ""
